    "cost_closing_html":  "About \u003cstrong\u003e${{cost_ps}} a bowl\u003c/strong\u003e (at everyday cost) for grilled pork noodle bowls with all the herbs and peanuts. The Vietnamese restaurant version is $14, and it comes with less pork than you just portioned.",
    "shop_smart":  ["Pork loin is the value cut here across 5.25 pounds, one of the cheapest per-pound proteins in the case. Sliced thin and marinated, it eats like the expensive stuff.","Fish sauce does double duty as marinade and dressing base. One bottle covers this batch and many more, so the per-batch cost is tiny.","Buy whole cucumbers and carrots and cut them yourself. The pre-cut versions cost two to three times more per pound for someone else\u0027s ten minutes of knife work."],
    "make_it":  [
                    "Weigh your empty mixing pot and write the number down for portioning later.",
                    "Slice the pork loin into thin strips. Marinate it in about a third of the fish sauce and a third of the sugar with the salt and black pepper for at least 20 minutes.",
                    "Make the nuoc cham dressing: stir the remaining fish sauce and sugar with the lime juice and about 2 cups of warm water until the sugar dissolves. Taste it, it should hit sweet, salty, and sour in that order.",
                    "Cook the rice noodles per the package, drain, and rinse under cold water. These bowls are meant to be eaten cool or room temperature.",
                    "Sear the pork in a very hot pan in batches, letting it char at the edges, until cooked through to 145F.",
                    "Slice the cucumbers, shred the carrots, and rough-chop the mint and cilantro.",
                    "Build bowls: noodles down, pork on top, vegetables and herbs around it, peanuts over everything. Pack the nuoc cham separately and pour it on when you eat."
                ],
